# Structure from Motion: photographs in, a 3D reconstruction out.
#
# Drop a folder of images on the palette as a GROUP -- hold ALT while dropping
# several -- then right-click the group and "Sort by filename" so the order is
# the capture order. The matchers chain against neighbours, so order matters.
#
# WHAT EACH STAGE DOES, because the chain is long and every stage solves a
# genuinely different problem:
#
#   detect_akaze     finds features. AKAZE because it measures the most
#                    repeatable of the detectors here (96% on the stock
#                    fixture against SIFT's 70%), and repeatability is the
#                    whole game: a point that is not found again in the next
#                    frame contributes nothing.
#
#   match_ann        pairs them. `chain` matches each frame against its
#                    neighbours rather than against frame 0, and `window` says
#                    how many neighbours. More than one is what gives the view
#                    graph loops, and loops are what let rotation averaging
#                    remove drift instead of merely accumulating it.
#
#   relative_pose    the ESSENTIAL matrix for each pair, and the relative
#                    rotation inside it. This is the stage that turns pixel
#                    correspondences into 3D geometry.
#
#                    `fov` is the most consequential setting in the script.
#                    The essential matrix is only an essential matrix in
#                    CALIBRATED coordinates, so a wrong field of view biases
#                    every recovered rotation. Read it off the lens where you
#                    can; sweep it and watch the rotation residual where you
#                    cannot.
#
#   build_tracks     chains pairwise matches into TRACKS -- one 3D point and
#                    every image that saw it -- by union-find. Global SfM needs
#                    the whole correspondence graph before it can solve
#                    anything, which is why this is a stage rather than a
#                    by-product the way it is in incremental pipelines.
#
#   rotation_average one global orientation per camera, consistent with every
#                    relative rotation at once. L1-IRLS reweights an edge by
#                    how far it disagrees with the rest of the graph, so a
#                    mismatched pair that survived RANSAC stops pulling every
#                    camera connected through it.
#
#   global_position  where the cameras ARE. Two views fix a translation only in
#                    DIRECTION, never in length, so this has to recover the
#                    consistent set of lengths from the graph as a whole. The
#                    joint method solves for camera and point positions
#                    together, which stays determined when the cameras are
#                    collinear -- a dolly or a straight transect -- where
#                    classic translation averaging is ill-posed.
#
#   triangulate      3D points from the solved cameras, with three rejections:
#                    too little parallax (the depth would be arbitrary), behind
#                    a camera, or reprojecting too far from where it was seen.
#
#   bundle_adjust_sfm  moves EVERYTHING at once -- cameras, points, focal --
#                    to minimise reprojection error in pixels. That is the only
#                    measure that says a reconstruction is right; every stage
#                    before it minimises a proxy.
#
# READING THE STATUS LINE, in the order things go wrong:
#
#   * matches per pair below ~100: the view graph is too thin to average.
#   * mean track length near 2: nothing is chaining, so every point is a bare
#     pair and the geometry has no redundancy.
#   * rotation residual above a degree or two: some edges are wrong. It climbs
#     with the matching window, because wide baselines share less and the
#     eight-point algorithm is weaker there.
#   * reprojection rms after bundle: under a pixel is good.

frames = image("group")

# Downscale first. A detector's thresholds were calibrated around a megapixel;
# on a 20 MP frame the same settings find tens of thousands of features on
# texture too fine to repeat between views. Every reference pipeline does this.
#
# 0.4 RATHER THAN 0.5, because at 0.5 the detector still hits its feature cap
# and what it discards is decided by RESPONSE RANK, which is not spatially
# uniform: the strongest extrema cluster on high-contrast detail and the flat
# regions that carry most of the frame get nothing. Measured on fountain-P11,
# the scale sweep peaks here: 3102 points at 0.3, 3238 at 0.4, 2602 at 0.5 --
# denser both ways, because 0.3 starts losing real detail.
#
# DO NOT SIMPLY TURN THIS OFF. At full resolution the reconstruction COLLAPSES
# -- 357 points against 2602 at 0.5 -- and the cause is the same cap: AKAZE
# finds far more than max_features at 3072 px, so nearly everything is thrown
# away and what survives is concentrated on the sharpest relief. Raising the
# cap to 30000 takes the tracks from 625 to 2785, which confirms it. The real
# fix is a spatially-aware cap (best N per grid cell, as ORB does) rather than
# a global response rank; until that exists, downscale.
small = params( resize, scale = 0.4 )( frames )

# MORE FEATURES THAN THE DETECTOR'S DEFAULT, because 5000 is a cap tuned for
# a single image and SfM is not that.
#
# A detector that hits its cap is discarding candidates, and what it discards
# is decided by response ranking rather than by which points the NEXT frame
# will also find. Measured on castle-P19 at 1536 px, window 1, fov 58:
#
#    cap     inliers/pair  cameras  points  ray residual
#    5000        200        16/19     618      3.30 deg
#   10000        537        19/19    1120      2.95 deg
#   20000       1063        19/19    1506      3.40 deg
#
# 10000 is the knee. The jump from 5000 is what registers all 19 cameras;
# going on to 20000 doubles the matcher's work for more points that are no
# better placed -- the ray residual gets WORSE, because the extra features
# are low-response texture that matches ambiguously.
#
# The cost is quadratic in the matcher, so this is the first control to lower
# if a large set is slow.
feat  = params( detect_akaze, max_features = 10000 )( small )

# CHAIN IS NOT THE DEFAULT, and leaving it off is the single easiest way to
# get nothing out of this script.
#
# Without it every frame matches against frame 0, which is right for a bracket
# of one scene and useless for a walk-around: frame 18 and frame 0 of a circuit
# share no view at all. Measured on castle-P19 with chain off, 17 of 18 pairs
# were rejected for want of correspondences and triangulation had nothing left
# to work with.
#
# window = 2 MATCHES EACH FRAME TO THE TWO BEFORE IT, which is what gives a
# track more than two views -- and the number of views per track is what
# decides whether the depths are any good.
#
# A two-view point is a bare ray intersection with nothing to contradict it,
# and at triangulate's 2-degree parallax threshold its DEPTH uncertainty is 29
# times its lateral uncertainty. Half a pixel of match error then becomes a
# large depth error, and a flat wall reconstructs as several parallel sheets at
# different depths -- which is exactly how this presented on fountain-P11,
# looking down at a brick wall that had become three walls.
#
# Measured there: window 2 removes most of the phantom planes.
#
# THIS IS NOT UNIVERSALLY BETTER. On castle-P19 the rotation residual climbs
# with the window (0, 4 and 15 degrees at 1, 2 and 3) because that capture
# turns ~22 degrees per frame, so frames two apart share very little and the
# extra edges are mostly bad. fountain-P11 turns ~12 degrees per frame and the
# skip edges are good. Match the window to how fast the camera turns: check
# `mean rotation` in the relative_pose report, and if frames two apart are more
# than about 30 degrees apart, stay at 1.
pairs = params( match_ann, chain = 1, window = 2 )( feat )

# NO align_features here, unlike the panorama script. It fits a homography,
# which describes a pure camera ROTATION -- exactly what SfM does not have.
# relative_pose runs its own RANSAC against the essential matrix instead, and
# writes those inliers back for build_tracks.
# FOV IS THE MOST CONSEQUENTIAL NUMBER IN THIS SCRIPT, and a guess here biases
# every recovered rotation.
#
# HOW TO FIND IT, since guessing from the lens is unreliable: run the chain and
# read `focal refined to fov` off the bundle_adjust_sfm report. Bundle
# adjustment solves for the focal against reprojection error in pixels, so
# where it settles is a measurement rather than an assumption. On castle-P19 it
# converges to 50.0 degrees from starting guesses of 36, 45, 52 and 58 -- four
# independent starts landing on the same value, which is what makes it
# trustworthy. Set fov_deg to that and the upstream stages stop fighting it.
#
# An earlier version of this script said 36 degrees, reasoning from a Canon D60
# APS-C sensor and an assumed 35 mm lens. The measurement says the lens was
# nearer 24 mm. Reasoning about the equipment was the wrong move when the
# pipeline can measure it.
#
# DO NOT TUNE THIS BY THE ROTATION RESIDUAL. It falls monotonically as the fov
# rises, all the way to physically impossible values, because too long a focal
# shrinks every recovered rotation toward the identity (34 deg at fov 30, 15.6
# at fov 115) and near-identity rotations agree with each other trivially. The
# residual rewards the collapse.
posed = params( relative_pose, fov_deg = 50 )( pairs )

# max_distance = 3 BECAUSE THIS IS A WALK AROUND A SUBJECT. The stages default
# to 20, which is loose enough for a scene shot from outside -- cameras
# clustered together, subject in front, every point legitimately ten or more
# camera radii away. Here the cameras ring the subject, so the real structure
# sits within a few radii and anything beyond is a stray.
#
# Measured on castle-P19: bundle adjustment returned a cloud 65.9 units across
# where triangulation had handed it one 13.3 across, from about 45 points of
# 1129. Minimising reprojection error alone lets a point on nearly parallel
# rays slide a long way outward while the error FALLS, so the check has to
# happen after the solve as well as before it.
#
# TIGHTENED FROM 4 TO 3 on fountain-P11, where a phantom cluster appeared off
# to one side that no camera was looking at. The limit is a multiple of the
# camera radius, and on that set 4 allowed points twice as far out as the real
# structure:
#
#   limit  points   raw Z extent   2-98% Z extent
#     4     7434        9.64            3.80
#     3     7258        5.76            3.21
#     2.5   6939        4.20            1.34   <- eating real structure
#
# 3 is the knee: the raw extent nearly halves while the robust extent barely
# moves, for 176 points. castle-P19 is unchanged at 4019 points either way.
# TRIANGULATE AND BUNDLE TWICE, which is worth more than any single parameter
# in this script.
#
# The first triangulation judges every track against cameras that global
# positioning placed and nothing has yet refined. Bundle adjustment then moves
# those cameras a long way -- rms 2.09 to 0.45 px on castle-P19 -- so tracks
# rejected for reprojecting badly were often rejected against the wrong
# cameras rather than for being wrong.
#
# Running the pair again re-judges them against the refined cameras. Measured:
#
#     round 1:  2889 points, reprojection median 10.6 px
#     round 2:  7659 points, reprojection median  1.1 px
#     round 3:  8324 points, reprojection median  0.5 px
#
# 2.6x the structure from re-running two stages that were already written to
# support it -- triangulate's header describes this explicitly, and COLMAP
# alternates the two until neither improves. A third round still helps but is
# clearly converging, and costs another full bundle solve.
# fov_deg HERE MUST MATCH relative_pose's, above. That stage normalises its
# correspondences with this focal; build_tracks fills in the camera slots every
# later stage reads. Two different values solve the reconstruction in one
# geometry and measure it in another -- and it is silent, because both are
# plausible numbers. This stage used to hardcode 50, which quietly overrode
# whatever relative_pose was told.
# min_length = 3 REJECTS TWO-VIEW TRACKS, and on a well-connected capture it
# is the single largest improvement in this script.
#
# A track seen by only two cameras is triangulated from one ray intersection.
# It reprojects perfectly -- it was placed to -- and its depth is whatever the
# match noise decided. Requiring a third view means the depth has to satisfy
# something it was not fitted to.
#
# Measured on fountain-P11 with window 2: the reconstruction's extent falls
# from 4.95 to 1.42 units, and the phantom planes and most of the stray
# structure go with it.
#
# DO NOT JUDGE THIS BY THE REPROJECTION RMS, which gets WORSE (0.93 to 1.18).
# That number is computed over the points that SURVIVE, and this filter keeps
# the hard ones -- a long track spans a wide baseline and is harder to fit --
# while discarding the easy two-view ones that fit trivially. A filter that
# removes difficult data always improves the average of what is left. I
# initially read that as the filter being harmful and was wrong; the cloud is
# plainly better on screen.
#
# NEEDS A CAPTURE THAT CHAINS. castle-P19's mean track length is 2.2, so this
# leaves it 399 points of 4019 -- it deletes the reconstruction. Check the
# len2 percentage in the build_tracks report: two thirds or less, use 3.
cloud = params( build_tracks, fov_deg = 50, min_length = 3 )( posed )
     => params( rotation_average )()
     => params( global_position )()
     => params( triangulate, max_distance = 3 )()
     => params( bundle_adjust_sfm, max_distance = 3 )()
     => params( triangulate, max_distance = 3 )()
     => params( bundle_adjust_sfm, max_distance = 3 )()

display( small, "frames" )
display( cloud, "reconstruction" )
